A||N||3||DaveL||Temperature Guage is Haunted||||11||
Z||000000||DaveL||05-15-2000||03:05 PM||||The temp. sending unit had been removed by a previous owner, and an aftermarhet temp guage installed on my 87 SE 2.5 DIS Fiero. Well, it said the temp was running at 160 degrees. Now I know that aint right caus I have an 180 degree thermostat. Well I bought the right temp sending switch for the guage and hooked it back up, and it seems to be working fine, but it reads in Centigrade instead of Fahrenheit. The conversion is multiply by 1.8 and add 32 degrees. This would indicate the engine running at about 220 or so, as the guage averages about 105 degrees centigrade. The fan is wired to run whenever the ignition is on. Does this sound like the right temp. range? I'm thinking of getting the A/C fixed, but I live in SW Fla. and it gets mighty hot down here. Don't really want to fry my engine, but I don't like frying myself either.||||reg||11
Z||000001||theogre||05-15-2000||10:12 PM||||Sound like the conversion is right. Get another temp gauge someplace. makes life much simpler.<P>IF the gauge is right.... and if it's off it's not very far off..... then with the fan on 220 is a might hight but not bad. Depends on condition of the cooling system and incoming air temp. Check and make sure theres nothing plugging up the front of the condenser or the radiator. Also... Hook up an ECM scanner and read the temperature it's getting. That is more accurate than the dash gauge.||||reg||1
Z||000002||DaveL||05-16-2000||08:00 AM||||I guess I'll just have to break down and buy one of those Auto X-Ray Scanners. Just hope the effect isn't anything like the "Scanners" I saw on the video, because my head may explode before I finish removing all the gremlins from my Fiero.||||reg||3
Z||000003||William Federle||05-16-2000||07:58 PM||||I was very confused with the temp gage in my 1984 SE until I got a mechanical temp gage from Fleet Farm. I screwed the temp sensing bulb into the hole in the head that the fan switch had been in. It turns out that what looks like the 1/4 scale mark on the temp gage is about 190 deg F. I let the temp rise to about 200 deg on the mech gage and the pontiac gage read just below the 220 mark. The pontiac gage was ok, the confusion is with the first mark. Since the bottom of the gage says 100 deg and the middle 220, you'd think the mark in between would be 160 but it's not. The reason I went through all this is that you might be dealing with the same ambiguity with the temp gage.<P>Good luck.<BR>||||reg||1
